<article class="env-lemma" id="0AH5">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AH5">Lemma <span data-tag="0AH5">16.14.1</span>.</a> Let $(A, I)$ be a henselian pair with $A$ Noetherian. Let $A^\wedge $ be the $I$-adic completion of $A$. Assume at least one of the following conditions holds </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$A \to A^\wedge $ is a regular ring map, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$A$ is a Noetherian G-ring, or 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$(A, I)$ is the henselization (More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0A02" data-tag="0A02">15.12.1</a>) of a pair $(B, J)$ where $B$ is a Noetherian G-ring. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Given $f_1, \ldots , f_ m \in A[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]$ and $\hat{a}_1, \ldots , \hat{a}_ n \in A^\wedge $ such that $f_ j(\hat{a}_1, \ldots , \hat{a}_ n) = 0$ for $j = 1, \ldots , m$, for every $N \geq 1$ there exist $a_1, \ldots , a_ n \in A$ such that $\hat{a}_ i - a_ i \in I^ N$ and such that $f_ j(a_1, \ldots , a_ n) = 0$ for $j = 1, \ldots , m$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0AH3" data-tag="0AH3">15.51.15</a> we see that (3) implies (2). By More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0AH2" data-tag="0AH2">15.51.14</a> we see that (2) implies (1). Thus it suffices to prove the lemma in case $A \to A^\wedge $ is a regular ring map. 
    <p>
      Let $\hat{a}_1, \ldots , \hat{a}_ n$ be as in the statement of the lemma. By Theorem <a href="/tag/07GC" data-tag="07GC">16.12.1</a> we can find a factorization $A \to B \to A^\wedge $ with $A \to P$ smooth and $b_1, \ldots , b_ n \in B$ with $f_ j(b_1, \ldots , b_ n) = 0$ in $B$. Denote $\sigma : B \to A^\wedge \to A/I^ N$ the composition. By More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/07M7" data-tag="07M7">15.9.14</a> we can find an étale ring map $A \to A'$ which induces an isomorphism $A/I^ N \to A'/I^ NA'$ and an $A$-algebra map $\tilde\sigma : B \to A'$ lifting $\sigma $. Since $(A, I)$ is henselian, there is an $A$-algebra map $\chi : A' \to A$, see More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/09XI" data-tag="09XI">15.11.6</a>. Then setting $a_ i = \chi (\tilde\sigma (b_ i))$ gives a solution. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>